<node id="paper_1">
  <data key="d17">Paper</data>
  <data key="d18">Constant inner potential DFT for modelling electrochemical systems under constant potential and bias</data>
  <data key="d19">Paper describing a constant inner potential method for grand canonical ensemble DFT simulations of electrochemical systems.</data>
</node>
<node id="model_1">
  <data key="d17">CatalystModel</data>
  <data key="d18">Electrochemical interface model</data>
  <data key="d19">Model system used for first-principles grand canonical ensemble DFT simulations of electrochemical interfaces under constant potential or bias.</data>
</node>
<node id="env_1">
  <data key="d17">InterfacialEnvironment</data>
  <data key="d18">Electrode potential or bias conditions</data>
  <data key="d19">Electrochemical interface environment characterized by experimentally controlled electrode potential or a biased two-electrode configuration.</data>
</node>
<node id="factor_1">
  <data key="d17">MechanisticFactor</data>
  <data key="d18">Constant inner potential parameterization</data>
  <data key="d19">Use of the local electrode inner potential, rather than the global Fermi level, as the thermodynamic parameter for electrode potential in grand canonical DFT.</data>
</node>
<node id="calc_1">
  <data key="d17">Calculation</data>
  <data key="d18">Constant inner potential grand canonical DFT</data>
  <data key="d20">grand canonical ensemble DFT</data>
  <data key="d21">[{"name": "electrochemical control", "value_numeric": null, "value_text": "constant potential or bias", "unit": null, "reference": null}]</data>
  <data key="d22">Constant inner potential (CIP) DFT using the local electrode inner potential as the thermodynamic parameter; compared with constant-Fermi-level GCE-DFT.</data>
</node>
<node id="obs_1">
  <data key="d17">ObservationClaim</data>
  <data key="d18">CIP and Fermi-level GCE-DFT approaches are equivalent for metallic electrodes and inner-sphere reactions, while CIP can also be applied to outer-sphere and biased two-electrode systems.</data>
  <data key="d23">computational_method_comparison</data>
  <data key="d24">CIP and Fermi-level GCE-DFT approaches are equivalent for metallic electrodes and inner-sphere reactions, while CIP can also be applied to outer-sphere and biased two-electrode systems.</data>
  <data key="d25">computational</data>
  <data key="d19">The abstract reports numerical and analytical studies demonstrating equivalence in the former cases and broader applicability of CIP in the latter cases.</data>
</node>
